t":"Created At: 2026-09-22T01:43:31+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-22T01:43:48+05:30\nThe search for \"best cybersecurity vulnerability detection dataset fine-tuning LLM 2025 2026 huggingface\" returned the following summary:\nFor fine-tuning Large Language Models (LLMs) in cybersecurity and vulnerability detection as of late 2025 and 2026, several high-quality datasets are available on Hugging Face. These range from broad cybersecurity knowledge bases to specialized instruction-tuning sets for vulnerability analysis.\n\n### Recommended Datasets for Fine-Tuning\n*   **[CyberLLMInstruct](https://huggingface.co/papers/2503.09334):** A prominent dataset containing ~55,000 instruction-response pairs specifically focused on cybersecurity tasks, including malware analysis, phishing simulations, and zero-day vulnerabilities. It is highly regarded for its applicability to real-world security scenarios.\n*   **[CyberSecurity-1M](https://huggingface.co/WhitzardAgent/CyberSecurity-1M):** A comprehensive, large-scale dataset aggregating diverse security content—including CVE records, threat intelligence, penetration testing, and defensive security materials. It is well-suited for general-purpose security fine-tuning and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) workflows.\n*   **[Primus](https://huggingface.co/collections/trendmicro-ailab/primus-67b1fd27052b802b4af9d243):** A pioneering suite of open-source datasets covering the full training lifecycle (pre-training, instruction fine-tuning, and reasoning distillation). It is specifically designed to improve LLM performance on security certifications and technical cybersecurity benchmarks.\n*   **[Trendyol Cybersecurity Instruction-Tuning Dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Trendyol/Trendyol-Cybersecurity-Instruction-Tuning-Dataset):** An active dataset frequently updated for instruction tuning, often used for training models to understand security-specific prompts.\n*   **[soham-dahivalkar/cyber-threat-intelligence](https://huggingface.co/soham-dahivalkar/cyber-threat-intelligence):** A specialized dataset used to train models like the `cyber-threat-analyst-llm`. It includes 5,000+ CVE records and 15,000+ instruction-response pairs focused on vulnerability assessment, remediation, and MITRE ATT&CK mapping.\n\n### Key Evaluation & Benchmark Datasets\nIf you are looking to evaluate your fine-tuned models rather than train them, consider these industry-standard benchmarks:\n*   **CyberMetric:** A benchmark of human-verified multiple-choice questions (MCQs) for assessing cybersecurity knowledge.\n*   **SecBench:** Focuses on exploit prediction, vulnerability classification, and defense mapping.\n*   **CTI-Bench:** Tests LLMs across various cyber threat intelligence tasks.\n\n### Recommendations for Success\n1.  **Prioritize Reasoning:** Recent research (e.g., *VulnLLM-R* and *VulnSage*) suggests that models performing \"chain-of-thought\" reasoning significantly outperform simple classifiers. If possible, use datasets that include step-by-step vulnerability analysis rather than just binary (vulnerable/not-vulnerable) labels.\n2.  **Combine Sources:** For robust performance, many developers use a \"mix\" strategy—combining large-scale corpora (like *CyberSecurity-1M* or *Primus*) for base knowledge and smaller, highly curated instruction sets (like *CyberLLMInstruct*) for task-specific alignment.\n3.  **Safety Awareness:** Be aware that fine-tuning LLMs on security data can sometimes reduce their built-in safety guardrails. Tools like the **OWASP GenAI Security Project** provide resources to help maintain model safety while optimizing for vulnerability detection tasks.\n\nYou can explore these and more by searching the [Hugging Face Datasets](https://huggingface.co/datasets) hub with filters for \"cybersecurity\" or \"vulnerability.\""}
